The '''Eternal Zongic Wars''' were a long era of warfare, dynastic consolidation, and fragmentation on the [[Jade Peninsula]], spanning roughly 150 BCE to 350 CE. The period began with the Zhongyuan-Donghai Wars, during which [[Zhongyuan]] expanded at the expense of [[Kingdom of Donghai|Donghai]] and absorbed the older states of [[Han]], [[Zhao]], and [[Qi]]. After the fall of Donghai in 285 CE, the peninsula was briefly reunified under [[Great Zhongyuan]], but the death of King Yuanxiong of Zhongyuan in 300 CE triggered the [[War of the Four Sons]], which shattered the imperial order and ultimately produced the successor kingdoms of [[Haizhou]], [[Nanyang]], [[Jiaozhi]], and [[Zhongyuan]].

Later historians used the term "Eternal" to describe the way the wars repeatedly linked conquest, civil war, and reunification across multiple generations. The era did not consist of a single campaign or dynasty, but of an extended cycle in which one great polity was replaced by another before breaking apart again.

== Background ==

By the second century BCE, the Jade Peninsula was divided between a maritime coastal kingdom in the east and a growing inland power in the central plains. Donghai controlled the northern coast, the sea lanes, and the principal harbors, while Zhongyuan dominated the interior and sought access to the coast. In the same period, Zhongyuan absorbed the older states of Han, Zhao, and Qi, bringing the central plain under a single authority and creating the military base from which later expansion became possible.

The wars that followed were therefore not a sudden eruption, but a long process of strategic pressure. Control of river mouths, coastal forts, and trade routes mattered as much as open battle. The peninsula's political order slowly hardened into two large systems: Donghai on the coast and Zhongyuan inland. Their contest shaped the rest of the era.

The era also overlapped with the [[Zongyuan-Miyagi War]] in the eastern Jade Peninsula. That conflict encouraged the establishment of the Sanese mercantile colony of [[Jingū Minato]], originally founded as a strategic foothold for continental exchange. Around 100 CE, however, the colony was lost as the balance of power shifted during the wider Zongic wars.

== Zhongyuan-Donghai Wars ==

The Zhongyuan-Donghai Wars began around 150 BCE and continued in one form or another for more than four centuries. Early on, the conflict was one of expansion and absorption. Zhongyuan's conquest of Han, Zhao, and Qi removed the inland buffer states that had once separated the two great powers, allowing Zhongyuan to project force directly toward Donghai's frontier.

Donghai remained difficult to conquer because of its maritime economy and naval strength. Its ships controlled the coastal approaches, its ports were fortified, and its commercial wealth allowed it to recover from repeated border setbacks. The wars therefore proceeded through raids, sieges, embargoes, and the gradual exhaustion of frontier districts rather than a single decisive clash.

By the third century CE, Donghai was increasingly vulnerable. The Haizei raids further weakened the coast, disrupting shipping and forcing the kingdom to divert resources to defense. In 285 CE, a major raid coincided with a Zhongyuan invasion. [[Queen Zhao]] was killed in the defense of [[Haicheng]], and Donghai's capital fell. The kingdom's collapse ended the old coastal order and left Zhongyuan as the dominant power on the peninsula.

== Great Zhongyuan ==

After the fall of Donghai, King Yuanxiong of Zhongyuan proclaimed the formation of [[Great Zhongyuan]]. His court treated the conquest not merely as territorial expansion but as the restoration of peninsula-wide order. Veterans were resettled, tribute systems were reorganised, and major ports were brought under direct royal supervision. Great Zhongyuan linked coastal trade to inland administration more tightly than any previous state had done.

King Yuanxiong's reign gave the era its high imperial phase. His government invested in roads, warehouses, and fortifications, and the court promoted a political vision of a single peninsula ruled from the central capital. Although the state was called "great", it remained vulnerable to dynastic succession, especially because the imperial family had expanded rapidly and produced multiple adult heirs with their own military followings.

The crisis came in 300 CE, when King Yuanxiong died and the court fragmented into rival blocs around his sons. The empire survived in name for another generation, but its real unity had already begun to unravel.

