== War of the Four Sons ==

The [[War of the Four Sons]] lasted from 300 to 318 CE and was the decisive civil war of the era. Each son claimed to be the legitimate heir of King Yuanxiong, and each attempted to preserve or reconstruct the imperial order in his own territory. The conflict was not simply a dynastic dispute; it was a struggle over the entire political architecture of Great Zhongyuan.

At first, the war remained a contest between rival courts. Hailin held the coast, Nanyan controlled the southern interior, Jiaozhe anchored the far south, and Zhongwei retained the old capital. Over time, however, the conflict became a full military breakdown, with shifting alliances, repeated defections, and local commanders choosing whichever prince could protect their land and trade.

By 318 CE, the war had effectively ended without a single restoration of central authority. Instead, the empire fractured into four durable successor kingdoms. The imperial title continued to carry prestige, but it no longer represented a unified state.

== Successor kingdoms ==

The aftermath of the war produced four states that dominated the peninsula for the next generation. [[Haizhou]] inherited the coast and its shipping wealth. [[Nanyang]] controlled the southern interior and much of the grain supply. [[Jiaozhi]] dominated the far south and the river systems that linked the interior to the sea. [[Zhongyuan]] retained the central capital and the symbolic legacy of the earlier empire.

Although these kingdoms fought one another intermittently, none was strong enough to reunify the peninsula immediately. Instead, the years between 318 and 350 CE became a second age of fragmentation in which the memory of Great Zhongyuan persisted while its institutions steadily dissolved. By about 350 CE, the old imperial order had been fully replaced by the rival kingdoms that later historians treated as the next stage of Zongic history.

== Legacy ==

The Eternal Zongic Wars left a deep imprint on later Zongic political memory. The era established the pattern of an inland unifier rising against a maritime rival, winning a peninsula-wide empire, and then collapsing into successor states. It also provided later historians with the model of a long historical cycle in which centralisation and fragmentation alternated over centuries.

The wars influenced military organisation, royal ideology, and the language of legitimacy. Later rulers of Zong drew on the memory of Great Zhongyuan when presenting themselves as restorers of order, while the successor kingdoms preserved local traditions that would eventually shape the later unification era. In this sense, the Eternal Zongic Wars were not only a period of conflict, but the foundation of the peninsula's later dynastic history.

